d but in  terms of this failure of communications  the suggestion appears to be that emails  were sent to out of out of use in boxes  somewhere in Whitehall I mean if that's  the case it does rather suggest that  someone at this end of the conversation  made a mistake and not updating their  details with the European Union this  this feels like speculation about what  may or may not have happened and I do  know that there has been no  communications error can you tell us any  more than about me exactly what the  communications error was then I don't  have any further information  tell you about that we know that that  has happened and that we will  participate we're participating when one  scheme we will participate in future  scheme so they sent the extent that it  makes good sense to do so what is the  important thing is to be making sure  that we are getting the pipi that we  need as we've just been talking about  we've had the delivery from Turkey  yesterday we're expecting more delivery  this morning expecting more deliveries  during the course of this week let's  focus on getting the P P P V that we  need into the country for our workforce  I see your wedding your your care badge  showing your support for those working  in the K and the social care sectors and  yesterday of course we had some fairly  dramatic figures revealing the extent of  the losses at the extent of the deaths  that have been taking place in our care  homes fivefold increase in deaths we  were you surprised by the scale of those  losses I mean it is really really hard  the situation in care homes and also for  those providing home care because there  you have some of the people in the  country who are the most vulnerable  who are often older and with underlying  health conditions so they do catch  coronavirus there are real risk of of  complications and and and at risk of  dying so we know though it's been  difficult to be able to get the sort of  accurate data and data that is specific  to the care sector as opposed to  including Hospital data but we do know  that there's people have been dying in  in care homes as the ONS data said  yesterday and I am working to make sure  that next week we'll be able to share  more up-to-date data but you know it is  I know it's really really really hard  for the families for people who have  lost their lives for those between care  and also the care homes themselves you  are coping and looking after residents  often that those that staff have known  for four months and not years I mean  it's really really hard it's really hard  um but just in terms of of those deaths  it was it was only in the third week of  this crisis that the government  identifying that there was something  pretty major something pretty bad going  on in our care sector care home  providers are telling Sky News that  there was a strategy of clearing beds in  hospitals and putting them into care  homes for the best part of three weeks  none of those were being texted we in  essence put the fox in the hen house we  allowed people who potentially had  covered 19 to associate with some of the  most vulnerable in our society I mean it  perhaps shouldn't be a surprise that we  saw fivefold increase in deaths and care  homes today so part of our strategy has  been to make sure that the NHS can cope  and then the NHS was able to look after  people who'd be admitted to hospital and  particularly to intensive care because  that is where we can save lives and that  involved people who were fit to be  discharged from hospital being  discharged with very clear guidance on  for instance making sure that there was  isolation and infection control all the  time we're learning about coronavirus  and how difficult in fact it is to  manage as an infection and we have  updated the guidance to make sure that  firstly well as testing increases we're  now able to test every single patient  who's being discharged from hospital if  they're due to go to a care home and 