e  symptoms so they won't be at work but  you're absolutely right that I know that  it is difficult and has been difficult  ready for care workers to get to the  testing sites because they're not  necessary nearby and not everybody has a  car at the moment we have 27 testing  driver testing sites and that is going  to increase up to 50 but hand in hand  with that and we have started we started  piloting and so some mobile testing for  that goes closer to where people are and  we're going to be sending up home  testing kits that will be delivered to  people so that will make it even easier  to be able to get the tests that you  need so these are the steps we are  taking to make sure that we can actually  use the testing capacity that we've got  in the country to test as many people as  possible and they will be gratefully  received when they arrive I'm sure but  just in terms of what we are learning  this morning the front page of The  Telegraph suggests that thousands of the  tests that have been carried out are now  considered to be invalid what is going  on there and what will happen to those  people who received  an invalid test so my understanding from  the clinical advisors is that some of  the early tests were evaluated and the  evaluation was actually they weren't  effective enough and that this is a kind  of a normal process when you are using a  test for an illness when when as we know  this is a nooner illness and we're  learning all the time  those who were tested with the test that  we you think isn't up to scratch have  been written to to let them know when  they will be offered another test is  that the potential then that we have  sent people to work on the NHS front  line to work in care homes with  coronavirus well in general we know that  the guidance has been to people if you  have symptoms to make sure that you are  isolating we have to make sure that we  look at the reliability of tests and  this has been also the whole debate  around the testing of people who don't  have symptoms for instance and one  reason why the testing is focused on  people have you do have symptoms because  we know that the testing is most  accurate when you have symptoms so this  is really really important not just to  test but to make sure we are testing  people effectively for the reason that  you say that you need to make sure that  it's given you accurate results on which  decisions can then be made Helen Whitley  always good to see you do continue to  stay well and thanks for being with us  thank you very much

